# ENP_WZ_Mapping
# Author: Boya Zhang, Sparkle Malone

# Part 1 Remote Sensing 
**Project Overview**: 
This project uses Landsat optical remote sensing observations to map the land-cover changes of the South Florida Everglades coastal wetlands since 1984. Land covers were classified into "Vegetation Zone" (VZ, mostly mangroves), "White Zone" (WZ, dead aboveground biomass), and "Transition Zone" (TZ, with optical signature between the VZ and WZ). About 1500 point samples were manually classified for eight years out of the 41 years time span (with ~6 years interval) using available high-resolution Google Earth imagery. We used an Machine Learning (ML) approach to spatially and temporally emulate the classification to the regional scale for the past 40 years. The results will be used for spatial modelling to track the trend of white zone expansion and mangrove migration.   

**Input data**:
1. Manually classified point samples of eight years (1986, 1994, 1999, 2003, 2009, 2013, 2017, and 2023) in the format of shapefile
2. Optical observations were obtained from Google Earth Engine (GEE), and the script was available in this repo


**File Structure**:
This repository includes both Google Earth Engine (GEE) and Python jupyter notebook code.
GEE code collects time series of optical observations. The Python jupyter notebook code compiles the optical data and manually labelled vegetation classification data into a machine learning framework to predict regional vegetation classification since 1984. 

To reproduce the results, one need to follow the six-step procedure. The order of steps were described as below.


Step 1 Generating shapefile for GEE to extract LS values
Step 2 GEE code to extract annual median values of LS data
Step 3 Sensitivity analysis for selecting the best VZ threshold
Step 4 Using the GE years to test spatial and temporal transfer learning
Step 5 Emulation process to generate land cover maps for the past 41 years
Step 6 Evaluate products for selecting optimal change detection threshold & Plot time series maps

The final output of time series raster (Change detection threshold 0f 0.6 with Closest GE years) is output for spatial modelling.

# Part 2 Spatial Modelling

Part 2 uses R scripts to define transect line, and generating intersection with the 41 years of classification maps. Plots were generated based on the intersection results. 

**Input data**:
Raster files of land cover classification time series produced by Step 1
Boundary lines, including coast line and inland line. 

**File Structure**:
Flow_Transect_Analysis.R is the work flow that calls all the functions
Functions:
  1. function_MakeVegetationZoneLayers.R polygonize the rasters
  2. function_Make_TransectLayers.R  make transects
  3. function_for_plots analyze the intersection results to produce plots

